 the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534357 17592186045417 true] [17592186420166 1072 #uuid "388312be-28d6-5dd3-90fa-f6561f195420" 13194139534357 17592186045417 true] [17592186420166 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x})+6\\alpha x{e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{-6x}.\n$$" 13194139534357 17592186045417 true] [17592186420167 1062 #uuid "2c91bcfc-99f4-5bfe-804e-7364e7854038" 13194139534357 17592186045417 true] [17592186420167 1063 #edb/ref 17592186308398 13194139534357 17592186045417 true] [17592186420168 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420170 13194139534357 17592186045417 true] [17592186420168 1062 #uuid "748c2caa-2098-5b2d-8ce4-acf93b07faac" 13194139534357 17592186045417 true] [17592186420168 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229643 13194139534357 17592186045417 true] [17592186420169 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420168 13194139534357 17592186045417 true] [